Siiiick.

I tried something like that before using this SO link <https://stackoverflow.com/questions/45114758/how-to-use-group-by-with-a-condition-like-if-then-else-and-apply-dplyr-philosoph> but I now see I was missing the extra curly brackets and R was upset about that. I do a bunch of stuff in the function before the group_by part, but I can just put that before the group_by part like...

group_by_switch <- function(data, switch){
  data_extra_steps <- filter(data, mpg > 15) #arbitrary filter for example purposes

  if (switch == T) {
    data_extra_steps <- data_extra_steps %>%
      group_by(carb)
  } else {
    data_extra_steps <- data_extra_steps %>%
      group_by(am)
  }
}

And despite seemingly using summary(n=n()) nonstop, I did not know about tally()...dopeeeee.

Thanks - and if anyone has another way of nesting the if_else inside of the group by, I would still like to learn that but otherwise Michael's solution gets the job done.

and actually now because of the combo of Michael's reply and re-looking at that SO link, I see how it works now and can do the unquote thing using !! ...nice.

Thank you thank you!!

John

